<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Theodoro</h1><p class="st-meta">1357 CE – 1475 CE (approx.) · capital Mangup</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Orthodox Greek principality in the mountains of south-western Crimea, the last remnant of Byzantine rule there until 1475.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, known as Gothia after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> who settled there in late antiquity, came under a local Greek-speaking dynasty in the 14th century. The fortress of Mangup, called Theodoro, became its capital. Its rulers may have been linked to the Gabras family of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Trebizond</a>, though their origins are uncertain.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The principality was a mountain state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/alans">Alans</a> and others, living from vineyards, farming and trade. Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-of-theodoro">Alexios I</a> fought the Genoese of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/genoese-gazaria">Genoese Gazaria</a> for the coast and built a port at Kalamita. Theodoro's princes married into the ruling families of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Trebizond</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Moldavia</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-of-mangup">Maria of Mangup</a> became the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-the-great">Stephen the Great</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1475 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, having taken <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/genoese-gazaria">Caffa</a>, besieged Mangup for months. The fortress fell in December 1475 and the princely family was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> and killed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> ruled the area directly, and Crimean Gothic, once spoken there, died out.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-theodoro.jpg" alt="Principality of Theodoro" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: Amitchell125 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caffa_and_Theodoro_2.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1357 CE – 1475 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mangup</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Alexios I, Isaac, Alexander</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gothia, Principality of Gothia, Mangup</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1416&amp;focus=principality-of-theodoro" aria-label="Principality of Theodoro on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5487 601 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Theodoro lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5807 857L6352 637M5807 857L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6352 637h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5807 857h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5807 857h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1416&amp;focus=principality-of-theodoro">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

